  - [1mExplicit only[0m — you hear nothing until you opt in. Clean model, but new users get an empty feed and miss things
    assigned to them, which reads as broken.

  [1mRecommendation: implicit + explicit follow.[0m The subscriptions table is cheap to build now and awkward to retrofit, and
  "unfollow this noisy task" is the first thing anyone asks for.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What makes a task one a user "cares about"? → Implicit + explicit follow (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Now delivery — where the notification actually shows up. This one adds external dependencies, so it's the easiest
  place to overscope.

  - [1mIn-app feed[0m (bell icon, unread count, notification list) — no external service, works while the app is open, and
    it's the durable record every other channel is just a pointer to. Required regardless of what else you pick.
  - [1mBrowser push[0m — reaches users with the tab closed. Needs a service worker, VAPID keys, and a permission prompt; also
    needs the in-app feed to exist first, since push notifications are fire-and-forget and users need somewhere to find
    what they missed.
  - [1mEmail[0m — reaches people not using the app at all. Needs a mail provider, deliverability handling, unsubscribe links,
    and digest batching to not be miserable. Largest add by a wide margin.

  [1mRecommendation: in-app only for v1.[0m Every other channel is a delivery adapter over the same event + subscription core,
  so building that core first costs nothing later — and picking email now means half this project becomes SMTP
  configuration.
